---
/**
 * The landing page.
 *
 * Every number below is computed at build time from `fixtures/` and the engine's own profile
 * definitions. Hand-written scores go stale the moment a dimension's defaults move, and that has
 * already happened twice.
 */
import { score, toScoreResultJSON, profiles, VERSION } from "prosemeter"

import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro"
import Scorer from "../components/Scorer.astro"
import Terminal from "../components/Terminal.astro"
import { fixtures } from "../lib/fixtures"
import { pct } from "../lib/view"

const allProfiles = profiles()
const thresholds = Object.fromEntries(allProfiles.map((p) => [p.name, p.thresholdDefault]))

const measure = (id: string) => {
  const f = fixtures.find((x) => x.id === id)!
  return score(f.text, { profile: f.profile, format: f.format, target: `${id}.md` }).fold(
    () => null,
    (r) => {
      const json = toScoreResultJSON(r)
      return {
        score: json.score,
        threshold: thresholds[f.profile] ?? 70,
        dim: (d: string) => pct(json.dimensions.find((x) => x.id === d)?.score ?? 0),
        grade: /median grade (-?[\d.]+)/.exec(json.dimensions.find((x) => x.id === "grade-band")?.detail ?? "")?.[1],
        dimensionCount: json.dimensions.length,
      }
    },
  )
}

const gamed = measure("choppy-simplistic")!
const jargon = measure("chat-jargon")!
const clear = measure("chat-clear")!
---

<Base
  title="prosemeter — deterministic scoring for prose"
  description="A 0–100 score, 15 per-dimension scores, and findings with line numbers and fix hints. Deterministic: same input, same output, no model calls."
>
  <section class="hero band">
    <div class="hero-split">
      <div>
        <span class="pill">pnpm add prosemeter</span>
        <h1>Deterministic scoring for <em>prose</em>.</h1>
        <p class="lede">Agents close the loop on code with compilers, linters, and tests. Prose has no such signal.</p>
        <p>
          prosemeter is one. Give it a document and a named profile. It returns a score, {jargon.dimensionCount} per-dimension
          scores, and findings with line numbers and fix hints — enough to revise, measure, and know when to stop.
        </p>
        <p class="cta">
          <a class="button primary" href="#demo">Score a document ↓</a>
          <a class="button" href="/use">Use it</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <Terminal
        fixture="chat-jargon"
        show={["grade-band", "sentence-simplicity", "clarity", "directness", "active-voice"]}
      />
    </div>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>{jargon.dimensionCount}</b><span>scored dimensions</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>{allProfiles.length}</b><span>built-in profiles</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>0</b><span>model calls</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>0</b><span>network requests</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint" id="demo">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>The same answer, two registers</h2>
      <p>
        Both documents answer the same question with the same fix. One scores {clear.score} on the <code>chat</code>
        profile. The other scores {jargon.score}. Toggle between them and watch which dimensions move.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Scorer />
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>A score is not enough</h2>
      <p>A linter says what is wrong. It does not say whether the rewrite helped, or when to stop rewriting.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose center">
      <p>
        Those are the two questions that make a revision loop terminate, and prosemeter answers both.
        <code>compareBaseline</code> diffs a result against the previous one and reports which findings resolved and which
        are new. <code>checkConvergence</code> reads a score history and returns one of five verdicts.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`const result  = score(draft, { profile: "readme" })
const delta   = compareBaseline(current, previous)
const verdict = checkConvergence([62, 71, 74, 74.5], { threshold: 80 })
// "improving" | "plateaued" | "oscillating" | "regressing" | "converged"`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The distinct stop reasons let a harness attach policy. <code>regressing</code> means revert to the highest-scoring
        earlier draft rather than push the latest one further.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>You cannot buy the score by shortening sentences</h2>
      <p>The obvious way to game a readability metric is to chop every sentence. Here is what that gets you.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-split">
      <div>
        <blockquote><p>The cat sat. The cat is big. The cat is red. It ran. It ran fast.</p></blockquote>
        <p>
          Perfect on {gamed.dim("sentence-simplicity")} for sentence-simplicity, {gamed.dim("clarity")} for clarity, and {
            gamed.dim("concision")
          } for concision. It still scores <b>{gamed.score}</b> and falls below the <code>chat</code> floor of {
            gamed.threshold
          }.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>grade-band</code> is the reason. It is a band, not a goal — full marks inside the target range, falling
          off on <em>either</em> side. This reads at grade {gamed.grade} against a floor of 7, so the dimension carrying
          the profile's largest weight scores {gamed.dim("grade-band")}.
        </p>
        <p>
          The design generalizes. Targets are ranges, so there is no single number to run away with, and anything gamed
          by chopping sentences is counterweighted by something that punishes it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Terminal fixture="choppy-simplistic" show={["grade-band", "sentence-simplicity", "clarity", "concision"]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Measured, not asserted</h2>
      <p>236 scored answers across three runs on <code>claude-opus-5</code>, plus a Sonnet transfer check.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="claims">
      <div class="claim">
        <b>32% shorter</b>
        <p>
          A style instruction against a no-instruction control on the same six tasks: 438 → 298 words, jargon 10.7% →
          8.7%, sentence-simplicity 50.2 → 69.9.
        </p>
        <p class="src">n=90 · 2026-08-03</p>
      </div>
      <div class="claim">
        <b>Naming the swap is what works</b>
        <p>
          “Be concise. Avoid jargon. Write clearly.” reaches 350 words and 10.3% jargon. Telling a model <em>which words
          to prefer</em> moved jargon; telling it to avoid jargon did not.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="claim">
        <b>Wording does not reach accuracy</b>
        <p>
          Two runs and one targeted mechanism test, no effect. The instruction written to fix an observed error failed on
          that exact error in 4 of 5 replicates.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="claim">
        <b>Read dimensions, not the composite</b>
        <p>
          Across seven variants the composite spanned 79.5–86.6 while the spread <em>within</em> one variant was about 15
          points. The dimensions moved 2–3x over the same data.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="prose center" style="margin-top:2rem;text-align:center">
      <a href="https://github.com/jordanburke/prosemeter/blob/main/eval/README.md">The method and the caveats →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>One engine, four ways in</h2>
      <p>The library is the whole thing. The rest are thin wrappers so an agent or a CI job does not have to be one.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag">library</span>
        <h3>TypeScript</h3>
        <p>ESM, typed, returns an <code>Either</code> so errors stay explicit.</p>
        <pre><code>pnpm add prosemeter</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag">cli</span>
        <h3>Command line</h3>
        <p>Exits non-zero below a threshold, so CI can gate on it.</p>
        <pre><code>prosemeter score README.md</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag">mcp</span>
        <h3>MCP server</h3>
        <p>Five tools over stdio. Each description teaches the loop.</p>
        <pre><code>npx @prosemeter/mcp</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag">plugin</span>
        <h3>Claude Code</h3>
        <p>The server plus a skill that says when to stop revising.</p>
        <pre><code>/plugin install prosemeter@prosemeter</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>What it is not</h2>
      <p>A deterministic prose metric is a proxy, and any agent optimizing a proxy will find its seams.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose center">
      <p>
        The bands blunt the worst of that. The honest framing matters more than the guardrail: prosemeter is a floor and
        a loop terminator, not a quality oracle. It tells an agent whether a draft cleared the objective bar and when to
        stop iterating. It does not tell you the prose is good.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also cannot check facts. Two eval runs and one targeted mechanism test found that style instructions move
        length and vocabulary by 2–3x and move factual accuracy not at all. A high score is not a correctness signal.
      </p>
      <p>
        And it measures how a document reads, not whether it makes sense to a particular reader — <a
          href="https://github.com/jordanburke/prosemeter/blob/main/docs/LIB_SPEC_context-completeness_2026-08-04.md"
          >a gap we have measured and not yet closed →</a
        >
      </p>
      <p class="src">prosemeter {VERSION}. Every score on this page was computed at build time.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
